      "title": "Flatten the beds, merge the labels",
      "citation": "PEP 798",
      "briefing": "The Gardens are laid out in beds, and the labels have been rewritten so often that the same plant appears three times.  Flatten one and merge the other -- each in a single comprehension, each unpacking.",
      "starter": "# The beds need flattening and the labels need merging.  Both of these already\n# work.  Neither is what 3.15 lets you write.  Rewrite each as a single\n# comprehension that unpacks -- no nested 'for', no itertools.chain.\n\nbeds = [['fern', 'moss'], ['ivy'], ['thyme', 'sage', 'rue']]\nlabels = [{'fern': 'shade'}, {'ivy': 'climber'}, {'fern': 'damp shade'}]\n\nplants = [name for bed in beds for name in bed]\nlegend = {key: value for label in labels for key, value in label.items()}\n",
