    "take-bytes": {
      "title": "The anvil that does not copy",
      "citation": "gh-139871",
      "briefing": "Buffering, parsing, decoding: read into something mutable, hand out something immutable, and pay for every byte twice.  3.15 lets a bytearray hand its bytes over instead of lending them.",
      "starter": "# Every one of these copies the buffer to hand it over, then throws the original\n# away.  3.15 lets a bytearray give up its bytes instead of lending them.\n\n\ndef frames(buffer):\n    \"\"\"Pull complete newline-terminated frames off the front of a buffer.\"\"\"\n    found = []\n    while (end := buffer.find(b'\\n')) != -1:\n        found.append(bytes(buffer[:end + 1]))\n        del buffer[:end + 1]\n    return found\n\n\ndef drain(buffer):\n    \"\"\"Empty the buffer and return everything that was in it.\"\"\"\n    data = bytes(buffer)\n    buffer.clear()\n    return data\n",
      "hints": [
        "The new method is on bytearray and returns bytes.",
        "With an argument it takes that many bytes off the front and removes",
        "them; with none it takes everything and leaves the buffer empty.",
        "buffer.take_bytes(end + 1), and buffer.take_bytes()"
      ],
      "points": 15,
      "reward": "zero-copy-anvil",
      "language": "python",
      "anchor": "whatsnew315-bytearray-take-bytes",
      "docs": [
        [
          "bytearray.take_bytes",
          "library/stdtypes.html#bytearray.take_bytes"
        ]
      ],
      "solution": "def frames(buffer):\n    found = []\n    while (end := buffer.find(b'\\n')) != -1:\n        found.append(buffer.take_bytes(end + 1))\n    return found\n\n\ndef drain(buffer):\n    return buffer.take_bytes()\n"
    },
    "math-integer": {
      "title": "A module for whole numbers",
      "citation": "PEP 791",
      "briefing": "The Numerarium counts in whole things.  Six functions in math have only ever taken integers and only ever returned them, and in 3.15 they have a module that says so.  The old spellings still work, and always will -- but they are soft-deprecated now.",
      "starter": "# These all work, and in 3.15 they are all soft-deprecated aliases.  The\n# number-theoretic functions have moved somewhere that says what they are:\n# they take integers and they return integers.\n\nimport math\n\n\ndef lattice_paths(rows, columns):\n    return math.comb(rows + columns, rows)\n\n\ndef largest_square(area):\n    return math.isqrt(area)\n\n\ndef beat(first, second):\n    return math.lcm(first, second) // math.gcd(first, second)\n",
      "hints": [
        "The new module is a submodule of math.",
        "It holds comb, factorial, gcd, isqrt, lcm and perm.",
        "from math.integer import comb, gcd, isqrt, lcm"
      ],
      "points": 15,
      "reward": "integer-abacus",
      "language": "python",
      "anchor": "math-integer",
      "docs": [
        [
          "math.integer",
          "library/math.integer.html"
        ]
      ],
      "solution": "from math.integer import comb, gcd, isqrt, lcm\n\n\ndef lattice_paths(rows, columns):\n    return comb(rows + columns, rows)\n\n\ndef largest_square(area):\n    return isqrt(area)\n\n\ndef beat(first, second):\n    return lcm(first, second) // gcd(first, second)\n"
    },
    "concurrent-tee": {
      "title": "Eight buckets, one spring",
      "citation": "gh-124397",
      "briefing": "Eight threads are dipping into the same generator.  Sooner or later two of them call next() at once and the Weir loses an item, or hands the same one out twice, or simply raises.  3.15 adds a wrapper that prevents it.",
      "starter": "# Eight threads, one generator, and a race.  Two threads calling next() on the\n# same generator can interleave badly enough to lose items, duplicate them, or\n# raise ValueError: generator already executing.\n\nimport threading\n\n\ndef harvest(source, workers=8):\n    gathered = []\n    lock = threading.Lock()\n\n    def pick():\n        for item in source:\n            with lock:\n                gathered.append(item)\n\n    threads = [threading.Thread(target=pick) for _ in range(workers)]\n    for thread in threads:\n        thread.start()\n    for thread in threads:\n        thread.join()\n    return gathered\n",
      "hints": [
        "Three new names in threading, and only one of them takes an iterator.",
        "Wrap the source once, before the threads start -- not inside each.",
        "shared = threading.serialize_iterator(source)"
      ],
      "points": 15,
      "reward": "weir-shuttle",
      "language": "python",
      "anchor": "threading",
      "docs": [
        [
          "threading.serialize_iterator",
          "library/threading.html#threading.serialize_iterator"
        ]
      ],
      "solution": "import threading\n\n\ndef harvest(source, workers=8):\n    shared = threading.serialize_iterator(source)\n    gathered = []\n    lock = threading.Lock()\n\n    def pick():\n        for item in shared:\n            with lock:\n                gathered.append(item)\n\n    threads = [threading.Thread(target=pick) for _ in range(workers)]\n    for thread in threads:\n        thread.start()\n    for thread in threads:\n        thread.join()\n    return gathered\n"
    },
    "removals": {
      "title": "Four graves, freshly dug",
      "citation": "3.15 removals",
      "briefing": "Every stone in the Crypt was a deprecation warning somebody scrolled past.  Four of them are in this module.  Bring it back to life on 3.15 without changing what any of it does.",
      "starter": "# Four pieces of perfectly good Python 3.14.  Every one of them is gone, or\n# fatal, in 3.15.  They were all deprecated first; nobody read the warnings.\n\nimport glob\nimport sre_parse\nfrom datetime import datetime\n\n\ndef compiled(pattern):\n    return sre_parse.parse(pattern)\n\n\ndef python_files(root):\n    return sorted(glob.glob1(root, '*.py'))\n\n\ndef release_day(text):\n    # The caller now passes dates like '2026-03-20'.\n    return datetime.strptime(text, '%m-%d')\n\n\ndef line_numbers(function):\n    return function.__code__.co_lnotab\n",
      "hints": [
        "Two removed modules, one removed function, one removed attribute,",
        "and one directive that is now fatal rather than merely wrong.",
        "sre_parse is gone; glob.glob1 is gone; co_lnotab is gone; %d with",
        "no year directive raises ValueError.",
        "re.compile / glob.glob(pattern, root_dir=root) / co_lines() / %Y"
      ],
      "points": 20,
      "reward": "crypt-lantern",
      "language": "python",
      "anchor": "removed",
      "docs": [
        [
          "re.compile",
          "library/re.html#re.compile"
        ],
        [
          "glob.glob",
          "library/glob.html#glob.glob"
        ],
        [
          "co_lines()",
          "reference/datamodel.html#codeobject.co_lines"
        ],
        [
          "strptime",
          "library/datetime.html#datetime.datetime.strptime"
        ]
      ],
      "solution": "import glob\nimport re\nfrom datetime import datetime\n\n\ndef compiled(pattern):\n    return re.compile(pattern)\n\n\ndef python_files(root):\n    return sorted(glob.glob('*.py', root_dir=root))\n\n\ndef release_day(text):\n    return datetime.strptime(text, '%Y-%m-%d')\n\n\ndef line_numbers(function):\n    return list(function.__code__.co_lines())\n"
    },
